This one started from a simple annoyance: recycling only works if the material gets sorted correctly, and asking people to tell glass from "other" plastic at a glance is a bad system to rely on. I wanted to see how far a small object detector could go at automating that first, error-prone classification step.

## Dataset

The training data comes from [TACO](https://github.com/pedropro/TACO), a public dataset of trash photographed in its actual surroundings rather than staged studio shots. I downloaded it, remapped the labels into six categories — `glass`, `metal`, `paper`, `plastic`, `other`, and `unknown` (unknown covers anything unidentifiable or unlabeled) — and rebuilt the folder structure YOLO expects.

## Getting the data into shape

Training itself wasn't the hard part — getting the dataset into a shape the model could actually learn from was. TACO's raw classes were unevenly distributed, so I used `albumentations` (horizontal flips, brightness/contrast jitter, small shift-scale-rotate transforms) to synthesize extra examples for underrepresented classes until they roughly matched the largest one, then capped the total at 2,000 images and split 80/20 for train/val. Going back through my own notebook, I can see that split-augment-reduce sequence redone more than once — re-shuffling, re-balancing, clearing the label cache and re-splitting again — which tells me the pipeline wasn't reproducible enough on the first attempt.

## Training setup

The model is a YOLOv8n — the smallest variant in Ultralytics' YOLOv8 family — trained locally on a CUDA GPU.

<SpecList items={[
	{ key: 'Model', value: 'YOLOv8n' },
	{ key: 'Image size', value: '416×416' },
	{ key: 'Batch size', value: '8' },
	{ key: 'Epochs', value: '50' },
	{ key: 'Hardware', value: 'CUDA GPU (torch 2.5.1+cu121)' }
]} />

## Results

On the held-out validation set, the final run lands at:

<StatBlock items={[
	{ value: '0.982', label: 'Precision' },
	{ value: '0.933', label: 'Recall' },
	{ value: '0.969', label: 'mAP@50' },
	{ value: '0.893', label: 'mAP@50-95' }
]} />

mAP measures how well the predicted boxes line up with the ground truth across confidence and overlap thresholds; the 50-95 figure is the stricter version, averaged over a range of overlap thresholds instead of just one.

I also set up a small [demo on Hugging Face Spaces](https://huggingface.co/spaces/Khunratanak/Waste_Sorter) so the model could be tried without cloning the repo or setting up a training environment.

### Where it breaks down

The confusion matrix shows where that number is actually coming from: five of the six classes land between 94% and 99% recall.

<BodyImage image={images['confusion_matrix.png']} alt="Confusion matrix across the six waste categories" caption="Per-class recall on the held-out validation set. unknown is the one weak diagonal cell." />

<Callout label="Limitation">

`unknown` drops to 81%, and most of what it misses gets predicted as background rather than as another material. That tracks — `unknown` is a catch-all by definition, so it has the least visual consistency to learn from, and the model tends to just not flag it rather than guess wrong.

</Callout>

## What I'd change

If I revisited this, I'd version the dataset-prep scripts properly instead of rerunning the same notebook cells with different parameters each time, and I'd either drop `unknown` as a target class or gather enough examples specifically for it to justify keeping it.
